WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:08.620
To understand what symmetry is let's draw an Isosceles triangle.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.360
It's a triangle in which two of its sides are equal.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.800
Now let me give you an easy task.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.380
You need to think if we can divide this triangle into two parts

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.040
such that both the parts match

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.560
exactly when they're folded along a line.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.140
How would you divide the triangle if this condition has to be satisfied?

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.580
Fold it along a line such that both parts formed match exactly.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.680
Pause the video and give it a shot.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.760
It's easy, and I'm sure most of you would have got it right.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.540
If we draw a line like this the condition will be satisfied.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:55.420
Yes, it's a vertical line which divides the triangle into two parts

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.060
which match exactly when folded along the line.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.080
If the part on the right is taken over to the left

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.840
we see that they overlap exactly.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.400
And that is why this shape is said to be symmetrical.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:12.440
The two parts match exactly when folded along a line.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.960
Now let me give you another figure.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:20.020
I draw the Isosceles triangle this way and ask you the same question.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.260
How would you divide the triangle into two parts

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:25.740
such that both the paths match exactly

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.740
when they are folded along a line?

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.620
Yes, we can draw a horizontal line like this.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.860
We get two paths which match exactly.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.720
When one is folded or flip it overlaps exactly over the other.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.100
Both these lines we see here

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.100
are called the lines of symmetry

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.100
or the axis of symmetry.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:51.640
This triangle has a vertical axis of symmetry as this line is vertical.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:57.620
And this one has the horizontal axis of symmetry as this line is horizontal.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:01.680
Now I want you to think about the next question I am going to ask you.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.540
Can a shape have more than one axis of symmetry?

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:10.240
If you look at just these two triangles, you must probably say no.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.280
Because there is no other line

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.140
which can divide these triangles

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.260
into two parts that match exactly.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:19.120
But the correct answer is that a shape

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.420
can have more than one axis of symmetry.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.740
Look at this rectangle.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.080
These two parallel sides are equal.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.720
These two parallel sides are equal ,

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.860
and all four angles measure 90 degrees.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.880
It will have a horizontal axis of symmetry like this.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:40.860
When one part is flipped over it will overlap exactly with the other.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.980
And it will also have a vertical symmetry like this.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:48.820
Flip this part and we see the perfect overlap.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:54.540
So a rectangle has a horizontal as well as a vertical line of symmetry.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:56.260
But wait..

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.940
Some of us may be wondering

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.300
if the diagonals are also the axis of symmetry.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.240
Let's say we have a diagonal like this.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.640
You can see that folding it along the diagonal does not give us matching parts.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.440
You can try this with a page from your notebook.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.900
Try and fold it along the diagonal

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:18.640
and you will see that the parts do not overlap exactly.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.520
Okay, here's another question for you.

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:25.200
Can a figure have more than two axis of symmetry.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.680
If yes then which figure?

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.180
Again the answer is yes.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.540
Take the example of a square.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:37.500
All its sides are equal and all four angles are right angles.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:41.740
It has the horizontal as well as the vertical axis of symmetry

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.620
just like the rectangle.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:48.920
But for a square the two diagonals will also be the axis of symmetry.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.920
If we fold it along this diagonal the parts match

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.900
and even if we fold it along this diagonal

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.060
these two parts overlap exactly.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.760
So a square has four axis of symmetry.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:06.100
Horizontal, vertical and the two diagonals.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.220
More than four, what if we have a figure like a regular hexagon?

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.200
A regular hexagon is a closed figure with six equal sides

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.580
and six equal angles.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:21.920
How many axis of symmetry does a regular hexagon have?

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.720
Pause the video and try to arrive at the correct answer.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:36.740
Okay, let's see. The horizontal and the vertical ones are obvious.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.900
These two diagonals would also be the axis of symmetry.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.220
Do you think there are any more?

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:47.040
Yes, the line joining the midpoints of these two sides

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.220
And the line joining the midpoints of these two sides

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.120
will also be the axis of symmetry.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.720
Folding the figure along any of the lines

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.900
will give us matching parts, six in all.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.680
And a circle on the other hand has infinite axis of symmetry.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.120
No matter how we draw the line passing through the centre,

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.660
the two parts formed will always fold perfectly along that line.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:14.620
Did you know that nature love symmetry.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:17.300
The best example of symmetry in nature

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.520
would be that of a human face.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.340
It has a vertical axis of symmetry.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:26.460
What we saw in this video is reflectional symmetry.

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:29.320
If one part is kept against a mirror,

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:32.200
its the reflection will give us the other part.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.980
Let's say we keep half the hexagon on this side of the mirror,

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.740
its reflection will give us the other half.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.040
Hence it's called reflectional symmetry.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.640
Is there some other type of symmetry too?

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:48.920
Yes, we will cover that in the next session.

